                        "form": "syntaxiques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2eeda271-0406-3599-92bc-55ce5898ac97",
                        "entry_id": "2ef86277-5088-31d0-be7b-2ad5e2a21170",
                        "glosses": [
                            "syntactic"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1279d39b-1dcc-462f-a185-44fa6f7783fc",
                                "sense_id": "2eeda271-0406-3599-92bc-55ce5898ac97",
                                "text": "Du point de vue des fonctions syntaxiques, les verbes d'état sont ceux qui définissent un sujet et son attribut.",
                                "translation": "From the point of view of syntactic functions, stative verbs are those which define a subject and its predicative.",
                                "english": "From the point of view of syntactic functions, stative verbs are those which define a subject and its predicative."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8f8d0413-2710-4983-aea5-df1fa13617f2",
                                "sense_id": "2eeda271-0406-3599-92bc-55ce5898ac97",
                                "text": "On verra en particulier que « -aille » est indifférent à la catégorie syntaxique de sa lexie de base et qu’il hérite de cette catégorie. (Marc Plénat, « Poissonnaille, poiscail (et poiscaille). Forme et sens des dérivés en -aille », 1999)",
                                "translation": "We will see in particular that “-aille” is indifferent to the syntactic category of its basic lexical unit and that it inherits from this category. (Marc Plénat, “Poissonnaille, Poiscail (and Poiscaille). Form and meaning of derivatives of -aille”, 1999)",
                                "english": "We will see in particular that “-aille” is indifferent to the syntactic category of its basic lexical unit and that it inherits from this category. (Marc Plénat, “Poissonnaille, Poiscail (and Poiscaille). Form and meaning of derivatives of -aille”, 1999)"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
        ],
